  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 316 AM EST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nAreas of light rain and drizzle associated with a positively \ntilted upper trough and cold front should exit eastern zones by\n12z. Today into tonight then features a transition to lake \nenhanced/effect rain showers thanks to ongoing cold advection \nwithin deepening north-northwest flow. This occurs on the \nbackside of a potent vort lobe dropping southeast through the \ncentral/eastern Great Lakes. More intense banding may set up for\na time in sw MI and nc IN later this morning through this \nevening as the prevailing flow becomes parallel to the long axis\nof Lake MI resulting in stronger frictional/thermal convergence\nover eastern Lake MI. Localized rainfall amounts may exceed a \nhalf inch in spots, best chances Berrien MI down into \nLaPorte/South Bend/Plymouth. Drying and subsidence eventually \ntakes its toll on inversion heights by later tonight and \nSaturday bringing an end to more organized LE rain. Cooler and \nsomewhat breezy early November wx otherwise today into Saturday.\n\nFlow regime flattens out Sunday into early next week with more \nactive westerlies shifting off to the north-northeast. Return west-\nsouthwest flow will usher in a milder pacific air mass with \nsunshine and warm advection resulting in a return to above \nnormal temps. Some degree of upper troughing and CAA then\nsettles back in for the second half of next week with near \nnormal temps and non-zero rain shower chances.",
